The fork's riscv.c comes from a later GCC, where TARGET_RTX_COSTS is
(rtx x, machine_mode mode, int outer_code, int opno, int *total, bool speed).
GCC 4.6 calls it as targetm.rtx_costs (x, code, outer_code, &total, speed)
(rtlanal.c rtx_cost): riscv_rtx_costs took the rtx code (PLUS, MULT, the
shifts, the comparisons: 49 to 59 here) for its `mode` and indexed
mode_class[] (FLOAT_MODE_P) and mode_size[] (GET_MODE_SIZE), 41 entries,
with it. It read whatever table the linker put after them, so every cost of
an addition, a multiplication or a shift depended on the layout of
insn-modes.o's .rodata. gcc-3's cc1 (built at -O1, tables in reverse order)
and gcc-4's (built at -O0, tables in source order) chose different
multiplication sequences and registers for the same input (19-gcc464 README,
rung gcc4, "Fixed point"). MIPS's 4.6 port, which this one follows, takes
the mode from X.

--- a/gcc/config/riscv/riscv.c
+++ b/gcc/config/riscv/riscv.c
@@ -1409,11 +1409,15 @@
   return COSTS_N_INSNS (2);
 }
 
-/* Implement TARGET_RTX_COSTS.  */
+/* Implement TARGET_RTX_COSTS.  GCC 4.6 passes the rtx code of X as the
+   second argument (rtlanal.c rtx_cost), not a mode; the mode is X's own,
+   as in mips_rtx_costs.  */
 
 static bool
-riscv_rtx_costs (rtx x, enum machine_mode mode, int outer_code, int *total, bool speed)
+riscv_rtx_costs (rtx x, int code ATTRIBUTE_UNUSED, int outer_code, int *total,
+		 bool speed)
 {
+  enum machine_mode mode = GET_MODE (x);
   bool float_mode_p = FLOAT_MODE_P (mode);
   int cost;
 
